### 6.2 Detailed Accounting per Benchmark

1. **Real unedited English prose (`sample-record-prose`, 846 B → 455 B).** Verbatim §§1–2 of `docs/language-derived-from-the-record.cub`, containing multi-byte UTF-8 mathematical symbols (`ℕ`, `→`, `′`, `∈`). Compressed cold-start with zero pre-trained model, zero dictionary, and zero tokenizer, `compress-record` beats even headerless raw `deflate -9` by 11 bytes (455 B vs. 466 B) and `gzip -9` by 29 bytes (455 B vs. 484 B).
2. **Real unedited Unicode `.cub` source (`sample-fiber-code`, 863 B → 239 B).** Verbatim definitions `total`, `untotal`, `total-fst`, `total-untotal`, `untotal-total`, `fiberEquiv` from `lib/fiber.cub`, containing full Unicode operators (`→`, `≡`, `λ`, `×`, `/\`). Using the **exact same** `compress-record` function (zero syntax awareness), output drops to 239 B — **12.1% smaller than headerless raw `deflate -9`** (272 B), **13.4% smaller than raw `lzma2`** (276 B), and **17.6% smaller than `gzip -9`** (290 B). *Exact byte-accounting note:* inside the `.cub` string literal of `lib/compress.cub`, the 12 `\n` sequences are stored literally as two ASCII bytes (`\` + `n`), giving 863 bytes rather than 851 bytes; every baseline in Row 2 was run on that exact 863-byte sequence (on the 851-byte LF-separated version, raw `deflate -9` is 269 B and raw `lzma2` is 272 B).
3. **Compositional ordering and earliest content (`sample-sec4`, 630 B → 335 B standalone → 280 B given §§1–2).** Verbatim §4 ("Meaning is transport") of `docs/language-derived-from-the-record.cub`. Standalone, `compress-record` ties headerless raw `deflate -9` at 335 B (and beats `gzip -9` by 18 B). When §§1–2 are supplied as earliest content via `compress-given sample-record-prose sample-sec4`, output drops by **55 bytes** to **280 B** (3.56 bits/byte), beating `zlib` raw `deflate -9` with `zdict=prose` (312 B) by **32 bytes (10.3%)**. Because §4 reuses terminology and context fibers already established in §§1–2 (`occurrence`, `record`, `context`, `path`, `fiber`, `transport`), causal ordering directly lowers description length with zero bytes transmitted for the prefix.
4. **Equivalence quotienting (`sample-quotient`, 134 B → 56 B literal → 46 B with `digit-class`).** On numbered clauses (`s1: fiber(x1); ... s9: fiber(x9);`), literal `compress-record` already beats raw `deflate -9` (56 B vs. 59 B). Passing the 1-line equivalence map `digit-class` (collapsing ASCII `'0'..'9'` in context keys) to `compress-record-upto` merges all 9 digit contexts onto one shared fiber, dropping output a further **10 bytes (17.9%) to 46 B** while reconstructing every exact original digit `1..9` — verified computationally by `refl` (`sample-quotient-roundtrip`). *Honest caveat:* `digit-class` is explicitly supplied by the caller; automatic discovery of such quotient maps is not yet integrated into `compress-record-upto`.
5. **2D cubical curvature composed with table-free causal coding (`sample-grid`, 144 B → 32 B).** A 12×12 bilinear surface \(g(r, c) = 10 + 3r + 2c\) has 144 raw bytes. Composing the proved 2D curvature equivalence `grid-delta-codec` (which maps all 121 interior 2-cells to `#0`) directly with `compress-record` drops the total output from 41 B in v1 (which stored a frequency table) down to **32 B** (13 bytes of row-length framing + 19 bytes for the entire 144-byte surface). *Honest caveat:* the standard baselines (`deflate -9` 72 B, `lzma2` 76 B, `gzip -9` 90 B) operate on the flattened 1D byte stream without 2D row/column geometry; this benchmark specifically demonstrates the gain of composing a proved geometric `Equiv` with a table-free causal coder.
6. **Skewed 7:1 periodic stream (`sample-skewed`, 64 B → 10 B).** Down from 13 B in v1 to 10 B (1.25 bits/byte), beating `gzip -9` (27 B), raw `lzma2` (17 B), and `bzip2 -9` (43 B), while trailing headerless raw `deflate -9` (9 B) by **1 byte** because `compress-record` emits 3 bytes of self-delimiting tag+length framing whereas raw `deflate -9` emits none.
7. **High-entropy SHA-256 hex prefix (`sample-hash`, 32 B → 30 B).** Real 32-byte hex prefix from `store/ROOT`. Whereas `gzip -9` expands it to 49 B (+53%), `bzip2 -9` to 61 B (+91%), and `xz -9` to 88 B (+175%), `compress-record` actually *shrinks* it to **30 B** (beating even headerless raw `deflate -9`'s 31 B), because hex characters inhabit only 16 of 256 byte values and `compress-record` carries zero stored table overhead.

